==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{root|ang|ine-pro|*ǵʰeh₁-|*h₁ey-}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*gān}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*gāną}}, from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*ǵʰeh₁-||to leave}}. The verb was defective in Germanic and may only have existed in the present tense.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|gān}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|gān}}

# to [[go]]
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/details/aelfricslivesof01aelf/page/522/mode/2up "The Seven Sleepers"]
#*: {{quote|ang|...hē wolde '''gān''' ūt of ðām porte...|...he desired '''to go''' out of the town...}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/details/aelfricslivesof01aelf/page/220/mode/2up?q= "Chair of Saint Peter"]
#*: {{quote|ang|...swā hwǣr swā hē '''ēode''' and hī ealle āstōdon ansunde ætforan him þe on ðām fænne ǣr lagon.|...wheresoever he '''went''', and they all rose up sound before him, who aforetime lay in the mire.}}
# to [[walk]]
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/details/aelfricslivesof01aelf/page/220/mode/2up?q= "Chair of Saint Peter"]
#*: {{quote|ang|...and hē lēop sona cunniġende his fēðes hwæðer hē cūðe '''gān'''.|and he leapt up immediately, trying his power of motion, whether he could '''walk'''.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=YD9HAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA378 translation] of [[w:Bede|Bede's]] ''[[w:Ecclesiastical History of the English People|Ecclesiastical History]]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Sē wer meahte unēaðe þurh hine selfne ārīsan oþþe '''gān'''.''|The man could barely get up or '''walk''' by himself.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n205/mode/2up John 6:66]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Siþþan maniġe his leornungcneohta ċierdon onbæc and lenġ ne '''ēodon''' mid him.''|After that, many of his disciples turned back and no longer '''walked''' with him.}}
# to [[enter]]
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n65/mode/2up Matthew 24:38-39]
#*: {{quote|ang|''On þǣm dagum ǣr þǣm flōde wǣron menn etende and drincende, and wīfiġende and ġifte sellende, ōþ þone dæġ þe Nōe on þā earċe '''ēode''', and hīe nysson ǣr sē flōd cōm and nam hīe ealle.''|In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day when Noah '''entered''' the ark, and they didn't know until the flood came and took them all.}}

====Usage notes====
* The expected present participle, ''gānde'', is very rare. Instead ''gangende'' is almost always used, from the synonym {{m|ang|gangan}}: ''Līf nis būtan <u>gangendu</u> sċadu'' ("Life is but a <u>walking</u> shadow").

